Installing Debian on scubed3 (debian-installer)
===============================================

partman-scubed3 adds scubed3 to the partitioner of the debian-installer, in
the same way as software RAID, LVM and encrypted volumes:

- "Use as:" of a partition has "base device for scubed3 (hidden encrypted
  partitions)"
- the main menu of the partitioner has "Configure scubed3 (hidden encrypted
  partitions)", next to "Configure software RAID", "Configure the Logical
  Volume Manager" and "Configure encrypted volumes" (without a base device
  it says how to make one)

"Configure scubed3" writes the partition table, fills the base device with
random data (or uses an existing scubed3 store, with its salt), starts the
daemon and shows a menu to create and open scubed3 partitions. The LUKS
volumes on them (/dev/mapper/scubed3-NAME) become disks in the partitioner,
the root file system goes on one of them. At the end of the installation
the installed system gets scubed3, its configuration and an initramfs that
opens the partitions at boot (contrib/initramfs-tools).

Files:

	build-udebs.sh			builds the packages (see below)
	partman-scubed3/lib/scubed3-base.sh
					functions (sizes, passphrases, LUKS)
	partman-scubed3/choose_partition/scubed3
					"Configure scubed3"
	partman-scubed3/choose_method/scubed3
					"Use as: base device for scubed3"
	partman-scubed3/init.d/scubed3	the LUKS volumes become disks
	partman-scubed3/update.d/scubed3_visuals
					"scubed3" in the list of partitions
	partman-scubed3/check.d/scubed3_check
					no swap or /boot on scubed3, other
					file systems on it need / on it
	partman-scubed3/finish-install.d/08scubed3
					scubed3 in the installed system
	partman-scubed3/templates	the questions (English only)

dev/installertest.sh tests the scripts in a simulated installer.


Installing on scubed3, step by step
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

1. Partition disks, manual: make a /boot partition (about 1 GB, ext4, not
   on scubed3: the kernel and the initramfs are read from it), and a
   partition for scubed3; set "Use as:" of the latter to "base device for
   scubed3". (An EFI system partition as usual.)

2. Configure scubed3:

   - the partition table is written
   - a new store: the partition is filled with random data (through
     dm-crypt with a random key, as partman-crypto erases a partition),
     a random salt is made and shown (keep a copy); an existing store:
     its salt and the number of iterations are asked
   - the menu: create partitions (name, size, LUKS yes/no, passphrase),
     and open existing ones. Create the hidden partitions you want now
     too; on an existing store, open ALL its partitions (in paranoia level
     2 and 3 the blocks of a partition that is not open are overwritten)
   - finish: the paranoia level during the installation (2 is
     recommended) and of the installed system; the unclaimed blocks are
     lent to the partition you install on (see below); the LUKS
     passphrases are asked

3. The partition with LUKS that got the lent blocks is a disk in the
   partitioner, with one partition: format with the default file system,
   mount point /. The other partitions with LUKS are disks too: a
   file system on them can be mounted at /home, /srv, ... (they need / on
   scubed3; the installer shows a note about them, see below). Finish
   partitioning and install as usual.

4. At the end of the installation (finish-install):

   - scubed3 in the installed system: the package scubed3 if apt finds it
     (put it in a local repository, see below), otherwise the programs of
     the installer are copied to /usr/local/sbin and the initramfs-tools
     scripts to /etc/initramfs-tools; cryptsetup, cryptsetup-initramfs
     (with CRYPTSETUP=y in /etc/cryptsetup-initramfs/conf-hook)
   - /etc/scubed3.conf (salt, iterations, paranoia level) and
     /etc/scubed3/initramfs.conf (BASE, by /dev/disk/by-partuuid if there
     is one, and the options of the daemon); the name of the root
     partition is stored only if you want (otherwise it is asked at boot,
     and you can have more than one root partition)
   - the other file systems on scubed3 (/home, ...): an entry in
     /etc/crypttab for every LUKS volume, with the partition as
     /run/scubed3/fs/NAME (a link to /dev/scubed3fs that the initramfs
     makes: for a source below /dev, systemd would wait for a device),
     nofail there and in /etc/fstab
   - update-initramfs
   - the lent blocks are given back
   - a note: keep a copy of /etc/scubed3.conf and the options

5. At boot the initramfs asks the name of the root partition, its
   passphrase, the names and passphrases of the other partitions (in level
   2 and 3 ALL of them, an empty name when done), confirms the paranoia
   level, and the LUKS passphrase of the root partition. After the switch
   to the root file system, systemd opens the LUKS volumes of the other
   file systems (from /etc/crypttab, it asks their passphrases) and mounts
   them; give the names of their partitions in the initramfs (also in
   level 1), otherwise the system boots without them (nofail). A key store
   (see Key store in the main README) can replace the passphrases of the
   initramfs with one passphrase: set it up after the installation, then
   update-initramfs -u (the LUKS passphrases of the other file systems are
   still asked by systemd).

   /etc/fstab and /etc/crypttab are on the root file system: whoever has
   the passphrase of the root partition sees that the partitions of the
   other file systems exist. Use them for data you can admit to; open
   hidden partitions by hand (scubed3ctl), never from /etc/fstab.


Install on a big partition, give the blocks back afterwards
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In paranoia level 2 and 3 a partition with a fraction f of the blocks of
the base device needs on average 1/f block writes for a block of its own
data, and the garbage collector copies less when a partition has more free
blocks. An installation writes some gigabytes: on a root partition with a
tenth of the blocks that is ten times as many block writes. Installing is
much faster on a partition that has (almost) all blocks.

But a partition cannot simply be made smaller afterwards: shrinking a
scubed3 partition cuts off the end of the file system on it, and the root
file system cannot be shrunk while it is mounted. So the installer LENDS
the blocks: when the menu is finished, the blocks that no partition claims
are added to the partition you install on as extra reserved blocks (scubed3
command resize-internal NAME BLOCKS RESERVED with both numbers increased).
Its size, and the size of the file system on it, stays what you chose; the
extra blocks are room for the garbage collector. At the end of the
installation they are given back (resize-internal with the old numbers):
the garbage collector of the installed system removes them from the
partition in the background, one with every write (in level 3 the clock
writes all the time, in level 2 only when the system writes).
"scubed3ctl p" shows

	(shrinking root, N blocks still to be removed)

until it is done; then the blocks are unclaimed again, for other (hidden)
partitions. Blocks that were never written are given back at once.

So: give the root partition the size it needs, with room to grow, not
more (it can be enlarged later, also while it is mounted: scubed3ctl
resize, losetup -c on the loop device under its LUKS volume, cryptsetup
resize, resize2fs); create hidden partitions with the sizes they need;
the rest of the base device is lent during the installation.

The same works by hand, outside the installer (scubed3 allows a resize of
a partition that is in use, as long as it does not become smaller):

	# scubed3ctl -c "info root"	(no_macroblocks=B, reserved_macroblocks=R)
	# scubed3ctl -c "resize-internal root $((B+N)) $((R+N))"
	  ... write a lot ...
	# scubed3ctl -c "resize-internal root B R"


Building the packages
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

On a Debian system of the release you install (the udebs must match the
libraries of its installer):

	# apt install build-essential autoconf automake pkg-config \
		libfuse3-dev libgcrypt20-dev libreadline-dev dpkg-dev
	$ contrib/debian-installer/build-udebs.sh OUTDIR

builds, with dpkg-deb (no debhelper needed):

	scubed3-udeb_VERSION_ARCH.udeb	the daemon and scubed3ctl (built
					--without-readline) and the
					initramfs-tools scripts, for the
					installer
	partman-scubed3_VERSION_all.udeb	the partitioner part
	scubed3_VERSION_ARCH.deb	scubed3 for the installed system (the
					initramfs-tools scripts in
					/usr/share/initramfs-tools)

The dependencies of the udebs: libfuse3-*-udeb, libgcrypt20-udeb,
libgpg-error0-udeb (from dpkg-shlibdeps), partman-crypto-dm (cryptsetup,
dmsetup, the dm-crypt modules, blockdev-wipe and blockdev-keygen). The
kernel modules fuse and loop are not a dependency (the names of their
udebs change with the kernel, and they are often in the installer
already): "Configure scubed3" loads them (modprobe, or insmod), and says
so if fuse is missing.


Getting it into the installer
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

- A custom installer image: put the two udebs in build/localudebs/ of the
  source of the debian-installer package and build the image (they are
  then on the image, and the dependencies come from the mirror or the
  image); or add them to an image made with debian-cd or simple-cdd.

- For testing, in a normal installer (expert mode): after the mirror is
  configured, in a shell (Alt-F2), with the udebs on a USB stick:

	# anna-install partman-crypto-dm libfuse3-4-udeb libgcrypt20-udeb
	# udpkg -i /media/scubed3-udeb_*.udeb /media/partman-scubed3_*.udeb
	# modprobe fuse; modprobe loop	(if that fails: insmod fuse,
					insmod loop)

  (the names of the library udebs depend on the release: see the
  Depends of scubed3-udeb). Then start "Partition disks" again. The
  running debconf of the installer may not have the templates of a udeb
  that is installed by hand (partman then says
  "partman/method_long/scubed3 doesn't exist"); partman-scubed3 loads
  them itself, from its copy in /usr/share/partman-scubed3/templates.

- The package scubed3 for the installed system: in a local repository
  that the installer knows (preseed apt-setup/local0/repository), or on
  the image. Without it, finish-install copies the programs of the
  installer into the target (they work, scubed3ctl without line editing);
  install the package later, it replaces them (remove
  /usr/local/sbin/scubed3* and the scripts in /etc/initramfs-tools then).

Preseeding: partman-scubed3/options (the options of the daemon, -m and -M,
default: chosen for the size of the base device), partman-scubed3/level,
partman-scubed3/boot_level, partman-scubed3/store_root_name. The
passphrases are always asked.


Limitations
~~~~~~~~~~~

- This was tested in a simulated installer (dev/installertest.sh: the
  scripts with stand-ins for debconf, partman's library and cryptsetup),
  NOT in a real installation yet; please report problems.
- Other file systems on scubed3 (/home, ...) need the root file system on
  scubed3 (the initramfs starts scubed3 only then). No swap and no /boot on
  scubed3.
- One base device. The base device cannot be changed after "Configure
  scubed3" (the daemon runs on it).
- The partition you install on needs LUKS on it (the LUKS volume is the
  disk in the partitioner).
- The key store is not set up by the installer.
- The shell of the installer must have 64 bit arithmetic (busybox with
  FEATURE_SH_MATH_64, the default), partman-scubed3 checks it.
- The questions are in English only.
- See also Limitations in contrib/initramfs-tools/README (no swap on
  scubed3, avoid running out of memory, level 3 limits the root file
  system too).
